  "answer": "To prove that the presence of air and water are essential for corrosion, an experiment can be conducted using three test tubes and clean iron nails. The procedure is as follows: \n\n1. Take three clean test tubes and label them A, B, and C. \n2. Place a clean iron nail in each test tube. \n3. In test tube A, pour some water and cork it. This test tube will have both air and water. \n4. In test tube B, pour boiled distilled water and add about 1 ml of oil on top of the water. Cork it. The oil will prevent air from dissolving in the water, so this test tube will have only water. \n5. In test tube C, add some anhydrous calcium chloride and cork it. Anhydrous calcium chloride absorbs moisture from the air, so this test tube will have dry air. \n6. Leave the test tubes undisturbed for a few days and then observe the nails. \n\nAfter a few days, you will notice that the iron nail in test tube A rusts, while the nails in test tubes B and C do not. This shows that both air and water are necessary for corrosion to occur. In test tube A, both air and water are present, which allows rusting. In test tube B, only water is present, but the absence of air prevents rusting. In test tube C, only dry air is present, and the absence of water also prevents rusting. This experiment clearly demonstrates that both air and water are essential for corrosion.",
